Trace: _redeemShares only takes the escrowed branch when isEscrowed is set by the caller (the vault burn path), and
  sharesToRedeem there is already bounded by the account's own share balance — the shares are burned in the caller, so
  an account cannot pass more than it holds without underflow revert. The ratio w.yieldTokenAmount * sharesToRedeem /
  w.sharesAmount is read from withdrawRequestManager.getWithdrawRequest(address(this), sharesOwner), i.e. the
  per-account WithdrawRequest struct, not a global pool. _postLiquidation calls
  withdrawRequestManager.tokenizeWithdrawRequest(liquidateAccount, liquidator, sharesToLiquidator), whose job is
  precisely to split the liquidated account's stored request: it debits the liquidated account's
  sharesAmount/yieldTokenAmount by the liquidator's portion and credits a request to the liquidator, recording the
  shared escrow in the TokenizedWithdrawRequest. After that split, the liquidated account's w already reflects only its
  remaining post-liquidation entitlement, so the pro-rata computation is over the correct (reduced) numerator and
  denominator — the second return value is discarded because the per-account struct alone is sufficient to express that
  account's fraction; the tokenized struct is the manager-side conservation ledger, and the manager (not the strategy)
  is the component that debits it in finalizeAndRedeemWithdrawRequest, which receives both withdrawYieldTokenAmount and
  sharesToBurn and performs its own accounting/validation against the account's stored request. Nothing in the shown
  file lets sharesToRedeem exceed w.sharesAmount for the caller, and the "over-claim" claim depends entirely on the
  unshown AbstractWithdrawRequestManager failing to debit the split at tokenization time and failing to validate in
  finalize — neither of which is demonstrated, and both of which contradict the documented purpose of
  tokenizeWithdrawRequest. The claim therefore rests on unshown code and an assumed-absent guard; the actual code path
  shown is internally consistent proportional math over an already-split per-account request.

